# Trivy ignore file — misconfiguration policy checks that do not apply to this
# reusable Helm chart. Only LOW/MEDIUM registry/namespace *policy* checks are
# suppressed here; every runtime hardening finding (UID/GID, capabilities,
# readOnlyRootFilesystem, seccomp, privilege escalation) is fixed in the
# templates, not ignored. The central trivy-fs gate targets CRITICAL,HIGH and
# there are zero CRITICAL/HIGH findings.

# KSV-0110: "Workloads in the default namespace".
# This is a reusable chart — the namespace is chosen by the operator at
# `helm install -n <ns>` time and is intentionally not hardcoded in the
# templates. Trivy renders with the default release namespace, so this check
# always fires for a namespace-agnostic chart. Not a defect of the chart.
KSV-0110

# KSV-0125: "Restrict container images to trusted registries".
# The trusted-registry allow-list is deployment policy, not a chart defect.
# Third-party images are pinned by immutable sha256 digest
# (quay.io/keycloak/keycloak, docker.io/library/postgres), which is a stronger
# guarantee than registry trust, and the first-party account-unification image
# repository is an operator-overridable value. Enforce registry trust via
# admission control at deploy time, not against the packaged chart defaults.
KSV-0125
